# 1  General Provisions

## 1.1  Rules Agreement

This Rulebook is the governing document defining the relationship between the organizer of 5EPC S1 2026 and all participants. By registering for or participating in the Tournament, participants assume the duty to familiarize themselves with these rules and may take part only after agreeing to all provisions herein.

## 1.2  Tournament Organization

5EPC S1 2026 is an all-online Tier 2 Counter-Strike 2 tournament organized by 5EPlay. The Tournament Administration comprises the Tournament Director, referees, match operations staff, proctoring and anti-cheat staff, broadcast staff and other authorized personnel, collectively referred to as the "Tournament Administration" or "Officials".

## 1.3  Tournament Administration

### 1.3.1  Compliance with Instructions

Players and accompanying personnel shall follow the instructions and arrangements of the Tournament Administration at all times. Refusal, delay or obstruction in carrying out official instructions may be penalized with penalty points up to disqualification.

### 1.3.2  Decisions and Appeals

The Tournament Administration makes all on-site decisions under these rules and may escalate matters to the Tournament Director where necessary. A team objecting to an in-match ruling may appeal only through its in-game captain, in writing via an official channel within 30 minutes after the match ends, attaching the specific round timestamps, demo or screenshots; late appeals or appeals submitted by a non-captain will not be accepted. The original on-site ruling and match result remain in effect while the appeal is under review. The Tournament Administration's ruling on any dispute is final.

### 1.3.3  Communication Channels

Official communication during the Tournament includes the 5EPlay match room, the official operations group [official operations group / voice channel, per the pre-event announcement], the officially designated voice channels, and separate communication between the captain (manager) and the Tournament Administration. Verbal agreements made on other social software have no effect. Each team must designate one captain as its primary contact, who must remain online and reachable by Officials throughout match time.

### 1.3.4  Authority of the Tournament Administration

To keep competition as fair as possible, the Tournament Administration may change or supplement the rules above and rule on circumstances not expressly covered; the Tournament Director may overturn any prior on-site statement or decision.

### 1.3.5  Privacy and Recording

Without the permission of the other party and Officials, players are strictly forbidden from privately recording, distributing or publishing voice conversations between players and Officials or between players and opponents (violators face a long-term ban). For the purposes of tournament management, refereeing, proctoring, disciplinary investigation and compliance archiving, Officials may record official voice channels, match rooms and proctoring footage as permitted by these rules; the retention scope is set out in Section 6.9.

### 1.3.6  Match Time

Match times are governed by the official schedule and operations notices. In circumstances such as force majeure, Officials may adjust or postpone matches and reschedule them in the manner that best preserves the integrity of the Tournament.

## 1.4  Rule Changes

The Organizer reserves the right to amend these rules without prior notice and retains discretion over circumstances not expressly covered. All rule changes are announced simultaneously on the official website and the public GitHub repository, with the latest version prevailing.

## 1.5  Confidentiality

Information within match channels and all exchanges with Tournament Officials are confidential. Without the written consent of the Tournament Administration, such content—including undisclosed schedules, matchups, veto intentions, and the discussion of investigations and penalties—may not be disclosed to any third party.

## 1.6  Broadcast and Viewing Restrictions

5EPlay holds the broadcast rights to every match of the Tournament. Participating teams may not refuse the broadcast of their matches through officially authorized channels and may not choose how their matches are broadcast; whether a match is broadcast is decided solely by the Tournament Administration.

- Without official written permission, teams and players may not stream their own matches and may not provide match servers, room passwords or observer slots to any third party.
- **Stream sniping is strictly prohibited:** while a match is in progress, players may not watch any live stream, delayed stream or third-party viewpoint of their own team or the current match, and may not obtain match information from any external source; violators are treated under the cheating provisions of Section 5.2.
- Players may not deliberately evade cameras or delay the broadcast through lag, stuttering or disconnection during the official broadcast.
## 1.7  Sponsorship

Team sponsors or partners may not involve pornography, gambling, prohibited goods or other content that could harm the reputation of esports or the Organizer; display involving betting or prohibited-goods sponsorship requires separate written official consent and compliance with applicable laws and regulations.

## 1.8  Team Standards

Teams and players shall provide necessary registration and verification information truthfully and promptly when requested by Officials.

### 1.8.1  Team Responsibility

A team is responsible for the conduct of its players, substitute, coach, manager and other accompanying personnel; the team may be penalized jointly when such personnel breach these rules. Teams shall cooperate with reasonable official arrangements for smooth match start, broadcast and proctoring.

### 1.8.2  Team Name

A team name may not carry undeclared extensions and may contain only the team name and any licensed title sponsor; it may not contain political, religious, insulting or other unlawful elements or elements contrary to public order and good morals. Officials may require a player to use a match nickname consistent with registration or matching official records.

### 1.8.3  Team Logo

Apart from a lawful title sponsor, a team logo may not contain brands, teams or trademarks unrelated to the team, nor political, religious or insulting elements. The logo must be submitted and approved at registration.

## 1.9  Prize Money

Prizes are paid to the team's corporate account within 30 working days after the Tournament, per the standards in the Tournament Overview. Where a winning team fails to provide payment information, invoices or other required materials accurately and on time, payment is postponed accordingly until the issue is resolved. Taxes on prizes are borne by the winning team; apart from the prize pool, no other compensation is provided.

## 1.10  Voice Communication

Online matches use the officially designated voice software and channels [official voice channels, per the pre-event announcement], which Officials may require to be changed. In each match, only the 5 starting players and 1 registered coach may be present in the official voice channel (coach communication limits are set out in Section 2.14); the substitute and other accompanying personnel must stay in a separate waiting channel and may enter only with official permission. Players must join voice channels under their registered nicknames.

## 1.11  Eligibility and Banning Standards

The following eligibility standards apply uniformly and transparently to all participants, with no discretionary exceptions:

- A player with a valid VAC ban within the 5 years preceding the start of the Tournament may not participate.
- A player who receives a VAC ban after taking part in any Valve VRS circuit event (including its qualifiers) may not participate.
- A player or team currently under an ESIC suspension, or with a verified match-fixing record, may not participate.
- A player or team permanently banned on a third-party platform for integrity reasons may not participate.
- A player whose VAC ban is older than 5 years and who has no other violation thereafter is not restricted.
**Non-exclusivity:** the Organizer does not sign agreements restricting any team or player from taking part in other events; the Tournament involves no exclusive arrangement of any kind.

**Non-discrimination:** all participants at the same stage receive equal treatment in technical support, preparation time and rule enforcement, and the prize and compensation structure is identical for directly invited and qualifier teams.

**Conflict-of-interest disclosure:** there is no relationship requiring disclosure to Valve—such as business ties, joint management, shared ownership, licensing or authorization, or loans—between the event operator/staff and any participating team.

## 1.12  Registration, Roster Lock and Substitutions

### 1.12.1  Registration and Slots

Each team consists of 5 starting players and 1 substitute; **only ONE substitute may be registered per team in this Tournament**. All players must hold a 5EPlay account in good standing and normal working order. Open registration is submitted through the official form; registration order is determined by the form submission timestamp and is displayed in real time in a public read-only table that anyone may inspect. After registering, teams must submit verification materials to the official email [official registration / verification email, per the pre-event announcement] within 48 hours; slots are not retained for teams that fail to submit on time. Open registration is capped at 24 teams; teams beyond the cap enter a waiting list in submission order.

### 1.12.2  Roster Lock

The complete roster (including the 1 substitute) must be registered and submitted before the Roster Lock date of August 31, 2026; in principle it may not be changed after lock. The initial seeding of registered qualifier teams is based on the August 2026 VRS ranking; a roster without a global VRS ranking may not be seeded above a roster that has one.

### 1.12.3  Original-Roster Requirement for Directly Invited Teams

In every match of the Tournament, each directly invited team must field at least 3 players who were on the roster at the time the direct invite was originally granted. A team unable to satisfy the "at least 3 original invitees per match" requirement loses that match by default.

### 1.12.4  Substitutes and Emergency Stand-ins

Ordinary substitutions may be made only between the starting players and the team's single registered substitute. An out-of-roster emergency stand-in may be requested only in genuine emergencies—such as internet outage, injury/illness or equipment failure—and where the registered substitute is also unable to play: the request must be made to and approved by the Tournament Administration before that match's scheduled start; the emergency stand-in must meet all Tournament eligibility requirements and may not be registered with another participating team; a player may represent only one team throughout the Tournament. Emergency stand-in cases are reviewed by the Tournament Administration on a case-by-case basis.

## 1.13  Schedule, Check-in and Lateness

### 1.13.1  Schedule and Match Start

Officials release the day's schedule no later than the day before each match day. Teams must complete check-in 30 minutes before the scheduled start, join the official voice channel and proctoring meeting, and complete identity, anti-cheat and peripheral checks; the veto process begins 20 minutes before the scheduled start (see Section 2.5).

### 1.13.2  Rescheduling

In principle all matches begin at the scheduled time. A team that genuinely needs to reschedule must have its captain or manager contact Officials no later than 24 hours before the scheduled start; Officials assess whether the grounds are valid and confirm feasibility. Unilateral rescheduling without official confirmation is void.

### 1.13.3  Lateness and No-show (Online)

Based on the scheduled start and veto times, the following escalating measures apply to teams not ready on time:

- Not ready within 5 minutes after the veto begins: 3 penalty points; if still not started by the scheduled start, a further 3 penalty points may be given for each additional 5 minutes of delay.
- Unable to begin the veto or match after 10 minutes of lateness: side-selection right is forfeited and the opponent chooses the starting side.
- Not ready to start 5 minutes after the scheduled start: 3 penalty points, and the opponent may choose the starting side.
- Unable to start 10 minutes after the scheduled start: deemed a forfeit—the team forfeits the whole match in a BO1, or the first map in a BO3/BO5.
- Still unable to start 30 minutes after the scheduled start: deemed to forfeit the entire series, and the opponent wins by official default.
The team must explain its lateness or absence to Officials; where the grounds are improper, an additional ban from this or future events may be imposed. Problems with a team's own network, equipment or account do not constitute grounds for exemption.

## 1.14  Slots and Roll-down

Direct invites are issued in order from China Region No. 1, based on the latest China Region VRS ranking on the invite date of August 18, 2026; invited teams that decline or are ineligible are passed over in VRS order until all 8 invites are filled. All directly invited teams sit below global VRS No. 12 (i.e., not inside the global top 12) to meet Tier 2 requirements. Qualifier slots are produced per the advancement rules; where a slotted team withdraws or is disqualified, Officials decide the replacement based on preliminary results or the waiting list, and the final interpretation of replacement rules rests with the Tournament Administration.

# 2  Match Rules

## 2.1  Game Version

Matches use the latest official release of Counter-Strike 2 at match time. Where Officials determine that the latest version is unsuitable due to a major defect or a last-minute balance update, the Tournament may roll back to a usable stable build; any update during the Tournament may likewise trigger a rollback, subject to on-the-spot official notice. Players must update the client before matches and may not delay the start on this basis.

## 2.2  Match Settings

Matches use MR12: the first team to 13 rounds in regulation wins; a 12:12 regulation score goes to overtime. Sides are determined during the veto, and the starting side of a deciding map is determined by a knife round. Server parameters are as follows:

| Setting | Value | Server Variable / Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Regulation rounds | MR12, up to 24 rounds | mp_maxrounds 24 |
| Round time | 1 min 55 sec | mp_roundtime 1.92 |
| Starting money | $800 | mp_startmoney 800 |
| Freeze time | 20 sec | mp_freezetime 20 |
| Buy time | 20 sec | mp_buytime 20 |
| C4 timer | 40 sec | mp_c4timer 40 |
| Tactical timeouts | 3 × 30 sec per map | Called by the team |
| Half-time break | 3 min (180 sec) | Side switch at half |
| Round restart delay | 5 sec | mp_round_restart_delay |
| Prohibited items | None | mp_items_prohibited "" |
| Overtime rounds | MR3, up to 6 rounds | mp_overtime_maxrounds 6 |
| Overtime starting money | $12,500 | mp_overtime_startmoney 12500 |
| Overtime tactical timeouts | 1 × 30 sec per team per overtime | Unused regulation timeouts do not carry over or accumulate |
| Overtime break | Disabled | No break between overtime halves |

## 2.3  Official Map Pool

The Tournament uses the current Valve Active Duty map pool; the baseline pool is below. Should Valve update the pool before the event, the official announcement prevails:

- de_inferno (Inferno)
- de_cache (Cache)
- de_anubis (Anubis)
